  <bioghist id="aspace_6b2d7b2b08758ccbc099d33d8d307473">
    <head>Biographical Note</head>
<p>Claire O'Connor (d. 2011) was the head publicist and Director of Operations for Peter Gatien's Limelight nightclub chain during the 1980s and 1990s. Initially hired as head publicist before the opening of the New York venue, O'Connor handled routine promotional tasks, booked musical acts, and managed media relations to generate press coverage for the club. As Gatien expanded his nightlife business, O'Connor was promoted to General Manager and eventually to Director of Operations for the New York, Chicago, and London locations. Throughout their ten-year business relationship, O'Connor also managed Gatien's daily schedule; handled his correspondence; and became an observer of the conflicts that led to the Limelight's demise. While employed at the Limelight, O'Connor married musician Adam Bomb on September 6, 1987 at the venue.</p>  </bioghist>
  <bioghist id="aspace_6876263abc5da4e83230e281d2d50ecb">
    <head>Historical Note</head>
<p>Opened in November 1983 inside a deconsecrated Gothic Revival church, Peter Gatien's The Limelight became an iconic part of New York City's nightlife scene. For nearly fifteen years, the venue attracted a mix of A-list celebrities and diverse club-goers, and became a destination for goth, techno, and industrial music. The nightclub's reputation devolved after the murder of club regular Andre "Angel" Melendez by promoter Michael Alig in 1996. During New York City Mayor Rudy Giuliani's quality-of-life campaign in the 1990s, the club was closed multiple times by police due to allegations of drug selling. The New York City Limelight location closed in 1997.</p>  </bioghist>
  <scopecontent id="aspace_e75d8d62bcfd03160830463064c54f51">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>The Claire O'Connor Papers (dated 1965-2010) documents the professional life of Claire O'Connor, focusing heavily on her career in public relations and promotion at the Limelight nightclubs in New York and London from the mid-1980s through the 1990s. The collection contains club ephemera; diaries detailing club operations and her address book; photographs of club events, notable guests, and interior and exterior venue shots; drafts and proposals related to her unpublished book, <emph render="italic">Lying with Dogs: Inside the Limelight Nightclub</emph>; and correspondence with Limelight owner Peter Gatien. Other materials include press coverage from 1987 to 1997 that documents the club's legal troubles, including Gatien's legal troubles and the Michael Alig murder case. The collection also contains a small amount of personal ephemera related to rock musician, Adam Bomb, O'Connor's husband until her death in 2011.</p>  </scopecontent>
